Start here
==========

This is a Windows port of litmus (https://github.com/notroj/litmus).
If you are picking this up cold, read these in order:

  1. README.md    what this fork is, how to build it, how to run it
  2. AGENT.md     how to use litmus to test a WebDAV server, and how to
                  read the results; also the JSON schema
  3. PATCHES.md   what was changed inside neon/ and why, needed before
                  you update the bundled neon
  4. This file    what is left to do

Layout:

  src/            the test suites, one .c per suite, plus common.c
  neon/           vendored neon 0.37.1, checked in, not a submodule
  neon/test/common/tests.c
                  the shared harness: result formatting, counters,
                  JSON output. Patched; see PATCHES.md
  win32/config.h  generated config.h, checked in so Makefile.w32 works
                  without autotools
  tests/          test harnesses (wsgidav.sh for Windows and Unix,
                  httpd for Unix only) and expected results
  Makefile.w32    the no-autotools build
  .github/        CI: both build paths, self-test, release

Before you change anything
==========================

Run the baseline and note the numbers:

  ./tests/wsgidav.sh --check

It must print "All suites match the expected results". If it does not,
fix that before doing anything else. Everything below assumes a green
baseline.

If you touch neon/test/common/tests.c or src/common.c, the default text
output must stay byte for byte identical. The way to prove that is to
build the tree twice, once with your change and once without, and diff
the output of every suite in both default and --quiet mode against the
same server. Do not skip this. It is the only thing standing between
this fork and silently breaking every script that parses litmus output.

Outstanding work
================

Worth doing, in rough priority order.

* The autotools build relinks every executable on every run. The link
  rules produce targets named "basic", "copymove" and so on, but MinGW
  gcc writes "basic.exe", so make never sees the target as up to date.
  Fixing it means putting $(EXEEXT) through the rules, the ALL_TESTS and
  TESTS lists, the install target and the litmus driver script. It is
  not hard, but it touches enough of the build to need careful testing
  on both Windows and Unix. Makefile.w32 does not have this problem.

* Test failure paths leak. Most tests build URLs with ne_concat and
  return through the ON* macros, which do not free anything. The three
  cases that left files on disk have been fixed (src/basic.c,
  src/lockbomb.c) because those littered the temp directory on every
  failing run. The remaining leaks are memory only, in a process that
  is about to exit, so they are untidy rather than harmful. Cleaning
  them up properly means a cleanup path per test.

* threadfn() in src/lockbomb.c returns early on error without calling
  ne_session_destroy(). Same category as above.

* --trace does not cover the lockbomb worker threads. They create their
  own sessions in threadfn() rather than using i_session, so the trace
  hooks are never attached. Tracing 400,000 lock/unlock round trips
  would not be useful anyway, but writing to one FILE from 20 threads
  would need locking, so this is deliberate. Say so if anyone asks.

* CI rebuilds the wsgidav virtualenv on every run. Caching it with
  actions/cache would save about half a minute per job. Low value while
  the job is this short.

* Upstream's TODO listed these, and they are still open upstream. They
  are test coverage rather than portability, so they belong upstream
  rather than here:
    - PROPPATCH may return 207 with an embedded 423; the lock tests
      check for 423 directly. Extending the PROPPATCH interface to
      return an ne_status per operation would let them check properly.
    - lock-null resource tests.
    - collection creation with a %20 space in the name.

Things that were looked at and deliberately not done
====================================================

* Merging the tolsen fork's tests, requested in upstream issue 8. See
  the "What was not changed" section of README.md for the reasoning.

* Connection reuse was measured, in case it was a performance problem.
  It is not: a props run makes 36 requests over one TCP connection. The
  slowest tests are copy_coll and move_coll in copymove, at about two
  seconds each, and that time is the server copying a collection, not
  litmus. There is nothing to win on the client side.

* Prefixing message bodies in the --trace output the way request and
  response headers are prefixed. Bodies are dumped by neon inside
  square brackets instead. That is better, not worse: PROPFIND and LOCK
  bodies are XML, so nearly every line starts with "<", which is also
  the response-header prefix. The brackets keep the boundary
  unambiguous.
